Six high‑leverage problem spaces where a more flexible AI "concept co‑pilot" can outcompete rigid rivals:  
1) Concept ideation & trade‑off synthesis — conversational, goal‑driven ideation that generates multiple orthogonal concepts, surfaces assumptions, and produces ranked trade‑off summaries and on‑demand variants;  
2) Parametric variant exploration & rapid feasibility checks — live parametric sweeps, constraint‑guided suggestions, uncertainty estimates, and interactive “what‑if” sliders keeping engineers in control;  
3) Manufacturability & compliance early‑warning — iterative guidance that flags likely tooling/assembly/regulatory blockers and suggests defeasible pivots with explanations rather than hard rules;  
4) Prototype & experiment planning — prioritized validation plans, BOM‑lite prototypes, expected signal sizes, and data‑collection templates tuned to engineering constraints;  
5) Cost/sourcing‑aware design‑for‑supply — integrates supplier capabilities, lead‑time and cost heuristics to propose material/process alternatives and sourcing tradeoffs; and 6) Knowledge capture & design rationale continuity — automatic documentation, rationale summaries, and dialogue‑based handovers that retain uncertainty and rejected directions.  
Cross‑cutting red flags: data quality and IP/NDA limits, engineers’ distrust of opaque advice (need for explainability and control), integration friction with CAD/PLM/toolchains, regulatory/safety liability, and the risk of over‑automating creative phases.
